  "context_pill": "Amalgamation Share Exchange Immunity",
  "example_summary": "M held 2000 shares in ABC Ltd, which amalgamated. M received 1000 shares in the new company, with a market value increase of ₹ 50,000. We examine if the Assessing Officer can tax this gain.",
  "source_provision": "# Statutory Framework: Amalgamation Share Exchange Immunity\n\n### Master Statutory Summary Matrix\n\n| Statutory Provision | Operational Scope | Statutory Mandatory Prerequisites | Capital Gains Tax Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Section 47(vii)** | Exemption from Transfer | Share exchange pursuant to amalgamation; Amalgamated entity must be an **Indian Company** | **Not Regarded as Transfer** u/s 2(47); Zero tax liability at exchange |\n| **Section 49(2)** | Cost of Acquisition | Shares acquired in amalgamated company under Section 47(vii) scheme | **Substituted Cost**: Cost equals original cost of shares in amalgamating company |\n| **Section 2(42A)(c)** | Period of Holding | Determination of Short-Term vs Long-Term status of new shares | **Tacking Provision**: Includes holding period of original shares in amalgamating company |\n\n---\n\n### Core Statutory Conditions Checklist\n\n* **Section 47(vii) Non-Transfer Protection**:\n  * The transfer must be in sole consideration of the allotment of shares in the amalgamated company.\n  * The **amalgamated company** must mandatorily be an **Indian company**.\n  * Any increase in market value of new shares over old shares at allotment is non-taxable.\n\n* **Substituted Cost & Holding Period Mechanics**:\n  * Cost of new shares equals the cost of acquisition of original shares in the amalgamating company u/s 49(2).\n  * Holding period of original shares is tacked onto the new shares u/s 2(42A) for calculating period of holding.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Judicial & Statutory Benchmark**:\n> An Assessing Officer cannot recharacterize a statutory amalgamation allotment as a taxable exchange under Section 2(47) if the conditions of Section 47(vii) are fully satisfied.",
